Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 19:26:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10949/11171] Correct a wrong diagnosis: the verdict guards are
 not punishing honesty

My earlier entry today claimed the verdict guards reject honest partial
self-classification and that omitting verdict_class is the only accepted route.
That was written from black-box probing without reading the function, and an
operator acting on it would have changed a guard that is not broken.

_verdict_is_untrustworthy has been enum-first since 2026-08-21. A declared
verdict_class inside the closed enum short-circuits the token lists, and exactly
one member retries: positive, circular_positive, null, blocked and disqualified
are all terminal, partial is not. The comment says so in plain words. It is
deliberate and it is the right default, because a partial result usually is
worth another attempt.

The real defect is narrower and survives: a capstone over a cascade-blocked
milestone is not partial in its own execution. It ran completely; its inputs
were retired. Retrying cannot change anything, so three attempts produce three
identical artifacts and a retirement. The fitting class is blocked, which is
already terminal and already accepted, and both exp6952 and exp6922 chose
partial when blocked was available.

That makes it an agent-side prompt fix rather than a conductor change, and no
conductor change should be made on the strength of the earlier entry.

Keeping the lesson with it: I established the three-way behaviour by probing the
function with crafted payloads and never opened it. Every probe result was
correct and the story built on them was not. A black-box result tells you what
happens, not why, and why was in a docstring twelve lines above the branch under
test.

